      {
        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 7,
        "titulo": "Which greeting for which person",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: «ciao» is for friends, family and people your own age. It means both «hello» and «goodbye», so Italians use it in both directions. STEP 2: with a stranger, an older person or in a shop, use «buongiorno» (from morning until the afternoon) and «buonasera» (from the afternoon on). English «good afternoon» has no everyday Italian equivalent: «buongiorno» covers most of the day, and «buonasera» takes over in the late afternoon. STEP 3: to say goodbye politely, use «arrivederci». With friends, «ciao» or «a presto» (see you soon). STEP 4: «grazie» is thank you, and the answer is «prego». «Prego» also means «please, go ahead» when you hold a door open, so it covers «you're welcome» and «after you». STEP 5: «per favore» is «please», and «scusi» is «excuse me» or «sorry» to someone you address formally. With a friend, use «scusa». STEP 6: pronunciation: «grazie» has a «ts» sound in the middle: «GRAH-tsyeh», not «gray-zee». And «ciao» is one syllable, «chow», as in the English word «chow».",
        "comparacao": "English speakers already know «ciao» as a casual word, but in Italian it is not for everyone: you would not normally say it to a stranger or to an older person. In English, «hi» and «hello» work with nearly everyone, while Italian splits the choice by relationship and time of day. «Prego» is the other surprise: it is not only «you are welcome», but also «please go ahead».",
        "exemplos": [
          {
            "ru": "Ciao, Marco!",
            "pt": "Hi, Marco!"
          },
