        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 5,
        "titulo": "Italian spelling is almost phonetic",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: the Italian alphabet has 21 letters. The letters J, K, W, X and Y appear only in borrowed words such as «jeans», «kiwi», «weekend», «taxi» and «yogurt». STEP 2: unlike English, Italian spelling is a very good guide to sound. Almost every letter is pronounced, and each vowel has one clear sound. STEP 3: the vowels are «a» (ah), «e» (eh), «i» (ee), «o» (oh), «u» (oo). They do not glide: «o» is not «oh-oo», and «e» is not «ay-ee». STEP 4: «h» is silent. It never makes a sound by itself, so «hotel» is «oh-TEHL». STEP 5: «c» and «g» have two sounds each, depending on the next letter. You will practice this in the next session. STEP 6: double letters are doubled in sound too, as you will see in lesson 3 of this unit.",
        "comparacao": "English spelling does not follow sound: «though», «through», «tough». Italian does. The price is that you must pronounce every letter and keep vowels pure. The one thing that does carry over from English is the alphabet shape: the same letters, but with the five foreign ones missing.",
